// Linux's `gcThreadToken` provider (#3420). See ../gcthread.bit for why this
// exists as a paired module now (Windows needs a different x86-64 register
// for the same job) and what the split costs on every target, Linux
// included.
//
// The payload below is byte-for-byte what used to live directly in
// ../gcthread.bit before the split — unchanged, not re-derived. See that
// file's header for the full argument (measured on hardware, not read off an
// ISA manual) for the AArch64 dual probe — TPIDRRO_EL0 first (Darwin's
// per-thread pointer, zero on Linux), falling back to TPIDR_EL0 (Linux's,
// masked because Darwin packs a CPU id in its low 3 bits) — and for why
// x86-64's `fs:0` load needs no such probe: it is the variant-II ABI TCB's
// self-referential word on both x86-64 targets this provider serves.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_gc_thread_token") fn gcThreadToken(): int {
  return asm {
    arm64 { 0xD53BD060, 0xB5000040, 0xD53BD040, 0x927DF000 }
    x64   { 0x64, 0x48, 0x8B, 0x04, 0x25, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00 }
    result arm64 x0 x64 rax : int
  }
}
